            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Spanish for the get-together, then the Spanish for the reunion."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: marcharse",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "marcharse — \"to leave, to go away\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Se marchó sin decir adiós. He left without saying goodbye."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Common in Spain; irse is used everywhere."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "marcharse is built on marchar, which was borrowed from French marcher 'to walk, to march' (EtymDB). The French verb is usually traced to a Frankish word for marking out, pressing with the feet."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
            }
          ]
        },
